=begin pod

=head1 NAME

Vips::Native - Raku FFI wrapper for libvips with bundled prebuilts

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=begin code :lang<raku>

use Vips::Native;

# 1. Smart-crop + resize to fixed output dimensions, saved as PNG.
smart-resize("input.png", "thumb.png", 128, 128);

# 2. Letterbox-resize to a fixed square + return raw 8-bit RGB pixels.
#    Aspect ratio preserved; gap filled with the chosen background.
#    Output is suitable to feed to ML inference engines.
my Buf[uint8] $bytes = letterbox-to-buffer(
    'photo.png'.IO,
    :size(448),
    :bg(255, 255, 255),
);
say $bytes.bytes;   # 448 × 448 × 3 = 602112

=end code

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<Vips::Native> is a Raku NativeCall wrapper around L<libvips|https://www.libvips.org/>,
shipped with prebuilt platform bundles so the common case (install,
import, run) doesn't require a working libvips toolchain on the host.

The module exposes two high-level helpers:

=item L<#smart-resize> — thumbnail-style: smart-crop to the requested
aspect ratio (using libvips's saliency / entropy / attention model),
then Lanczos3-resize, then save as PNG.

=item L<#letterbox-to-buffer> — ML-preprocess: scale-to-fit inside a
square canvas (no crop), pad the gap with a chosen colour, composite
RGBA over that colour, and return the raw RGB pixel buffer in NHWC
byte order. The preprocess pipeline image classifiers want.

The full (variadic) libvips C API is available via direct C<is native>
bindings under L<Vips::Native::FFI> — see the module's SYNOPSIS for
the bindings exported. Most of those go through a tiny non-variadic C
shim (C<src/vips_native_shim.c>) for ABI safety on Apple arm64 / Linux
aarch64, where Raku's NativeCall can't marshal C variadics correctly.

=head1 INSTALLATION

C<Vips::Native> tries three lookup paths at install + load time, in
order:

=item B<Prebuilt bundle (default)> — fetched from the project's
GitHub Releases at install time, sha256-pinned against
C<resources/checksums.txt>, staged under
C<$XDG_DATA_HOME/Vips-Native/<binary-tag>/lib/>. Bundles include
libvips, libglib, libgio, libgobject, the format loaders
(jpeg/png/webp/tiff/gif/svg/heif/avif/jxl/openslide/pdf, and magick
on Windows only) and all their transitive deps with C<@loader_path>
(macOS) / C<$ORIGIN> (Linux) / sibling-DLL (Windows) relocations
baked in, so the bundle is self-contained — and a C<THIRD-PARTY.md> +
C<LICENSES/> notices kit, staged beside the libraries (see
I<BINARY DISTRIBUTIONS AND LICENSING> below).

=item B<System libvips> — fallback when the prebuilt download fails
or no prebuilt exists for your platform. C<Vips::Native> probes for
an installed libvips itself and loads it by absolute path: first the
dynamic loader's own search variables (C<DYLD_LIBRARY_PATH> and
C<DYLD_FALLBACK_LIBRARY_PATH> on macOS, C<LD_LIBRARY_PATH> on Linux,
C<PATH> on Windows), then the platform's library directories —
including Homebrew's per-formula opt kegs, so an B<unlinked>
C<brew install vips> is still found — and finally C<ldconfig -p>'s
cache listing on Linux. It must be libvips 8.12 or later (Ubuntu 22.04
ships 8.12, Debian 12 8.14): an older one is refused when the module
loads, with the version found and what to do. Older distributions get
the prebuilt, which needs only glibc 2.17.
=begin item
On macOS:    C<brew install vips>
=end item
=begin item
On Debian/Ubuntu: C<sudo apt install libvips-dev>
=end item

=item B<Custom install> — set C<VIPS_NATIVE_LIB_DIR=/path/to/dir> to
point at a directory containing C<libvips.{dylib,so,dll}> and
C<libgobject-2.0.{dylib,so,dll}>. Caller takes responsibility for
ABI compatibility, and for that install's own environment
(C<VIPSHOME>, GIO modules); on Windows the directory is still made
the DLL search directory, since its libvips cannot load its own
dependencies otherwise. A C<libvips_shim> in the same directory is
used in preference to the staged one. A value that is not a
directory is ignored, by everything, and the staged bundle is used.

=head2 Supported platforms

=item macOS arm64 (Apple silicon, macOS 11 or later)
=item Linux x86_64 + aarch64 (glibc 2.17+)
=item Windows x86_64 + arm64

Every supported platform ships a working bundle with the varargs
shim included.

x86_64 macOS has no prebuilt: neither an Intel Mac nor an x86_64 Raku
running under Rosetta on Apple silicon (which reports itself as
x86_64). There, installing falls back to a system libvips, and that
libvips has to be x86_64 too — an arm64 one, such as Homebrew's on
Apple silicon, cannot load into an x86_64 process. Install an x86_64
libvips (MacPorts, or your own build) and C<Vips::Native> finds it in
the usual library directories, or point C<VIPS_NATIVE_LIB_DIR> at it.
The install says so when it happens. An arm64 Raku on the same Mac
gets the prebuilt.

=head2 Build-time environment

=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_PREFER_SYSTEM=1> — skip prebuilt download, force
system libvips at runtime.
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_BINARY_ONLY=1> — refuse to fall back to system
libvips; fail loudly if the prebuilt can't be installed (CI / pinned
deployments).
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_DATA_DIR=/path> — override staged-libs root
(default C<$XDG_DATA_HOME> or C<~/.local/share>).
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_CACHE_DIR=/path> — override the cache for
prebuilt archives and compiled shims (default C<$XDG_CACHE_HOME> or
C<~/.cache>; C<%LOCALAPPDATA%> on Windows).
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_CC=cc> — the C compiler for the varargs shim, as
a path or a name on C<PATH> (default: the first of C<cc>, C<gcc>,
C<clang>).
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_BINARY_URL=https://…> — override the GitHub
Release base URL (custom mirror).
=item C<VIPS_NATIVE_KEEP_OLD_STAGES=1> — opt out of the orphaned-stage
GC that runs after every install. Use when intentionally pinning
multiple versions side-by-side.

=head1 BINARY DISTRIBUTIONS AND LICENSING

C<Vips::Native> itself is Artistic-2.0. The prebuilt packs it downloads
are something else: binary redistributions of libvips and the libraries
it depends on, built by third parties and repackaged here unmodified
apart from their load paths. The only code of C<Vips::Native>'s own in
them is the varargs shim (C<libvips_shim>, Artistic-2.0).

B<The prebuilt packs contain GPL-licensed code>, as well as
LGPL-licensed code, code under the weak-copyleft MPL-2.0, and code
under permissive licences:

=item B<macOS arm64, Linux x86_64, Linux aarch64> — GPL: FFTW (which
libvips itself links), poppler (the PDF loader), x265 (an HEVC encoder,
through libheif) and LZO (through libarchive), all GPL-2.0-or-later.
FreeType is dual-licensed, GPL-2.0 or the FreeType Licence. On Linux,
the GCC runtime (C<libstdc++>, C<libgcc_s>, and C<libgomp>, the OpenMP
runtime LibRaw uses) is GPL-3.0 with the GCC Runtime Library
Exception. LGPL: libvips, GLib, cairo, pango, librsvg (with MPL-2.0
Rust crates built into it), gdk-pixbuf, fribidi, graphite2, libexif,
LibRaw, OpenSlide, libiconv, libintl (macOS), keyutils (Linux), and
libheif and libde265 (LGPL-3.0). MPL-2.0: NSS and NSPR (through
poppler), and the Public Suffix List built into libpsl.

=item B<Windows x86_64, Windows arm64> — GPL: FFTW and poppler.
Dual-licensed, GPL-2.0 or another licence: FreeType (or the FreeType
Licence) and zstd (or BSD-3-Clause). LGPL: libvips, GLib (with
proxy-libintl built into it), cairo, pango, librsvg, fribidi, libexif,
LibRaw, OpenSlide and libheif (LGPL-3.0). MPL-2.0: the Rust crates
built into librsvg — cssparser 0.37.0, cssparser-color 0.5.0,
dtoa-short 0.3.5 and selectors 0.40.0.

Every pack carries a C<THIRD-PARTY.md> and a C<LICENSES/> directory,
staged beside the libraries in C<$XDG_DATA_HOME/Vips-Native/<binary-tag>/lib/>
(installation refuses an archive without them). C<THIRD-PARTY.md> maps
every library file in the pack to the package or component it came
from, with its version, its licence and where its source lives, and
calls out the licences above; C<LICENSES/> holds the licence texts,
including the GPL-3.0 text the LGPL-3.0 incorporates, and the notices
that go with them (Lerc's NOTICE on macOS and Linux, and in every pack
the notices of FreeType's BDF and PCF font drivers). The Windows packs'
upstream archive carries only libvips's own licence text, so every
component's licence texts are taken from the source it was built from,
each checked against a pinned SHA-256 when the pack is built — the
components built into another's DLL (the mingw-w64 runtime and LLVM's
compiler-rt in every DLL, proxy-libintl in GLib's, the Rust crates and
standard library in librsvg's, Imath in OpenEXR's, uthash in
libdicom's) among them. A pack is not built if a package in it declares
a copyleft licence this section does not name.

B<Where to get the source.> Each pack's C<THIRD-PARTY.md> lists, under
I<Where to get the source>, the source archive of every GPL, LGPL and
MPL component in it, with the SHA-256 the build checked it against, and
the recipe that built it (the build script and any patches). The
MPL-2.0 code's source is also at its upstreams: NSS at
L<https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/security/nss/releases/> and NSPR at
L<https://ftp.mozilla.org/pub/nspr/releases/> (macOS and Linux), and
each Rust crate at C<https://crates.io/crates/<name>/<version>> — on
Windows L<cssparser 0.37.0|https://crates.io/crates/cssparser/0.37.0>,
L<cssparser-color 0.5.0|https://crates.io/crates/cssparser-color/0.5.0>,
L<dtoa-short 0.3.5|https://crates.io/crates/dtoa-short/0.3.5> and
L<selectors 0.40.0|https://crates.io/crates/selectors/0.40.0>; on macOS
and Linux, the versions in that pack's librsvg, which its
C<THIRD-PARTY.md> names.

B<Acknowledgements.> The licences of FreeType, of the IJG-derived JPEG
libraries (libjpeg-turbo on macOS and Linux, mozjpeg on Windows) and of
libultrahdr (Windows) ask the documentation of a binary distribution to
carry these statements, and each pack's C<THIRD-PARTY.md> carries the
ones for what it holds:

=item Portions of this software are copyright © 2026 The FreeType
Project (L<https://freetype.org>). All rights reserved.
=item This software is based in part on the work of the Independent
JPEG Group.
=item This product includes Gain Map technology under license by Adobe
(Windows).

Tables derived from the Unicode Character Database are compiled into
GLib, FriBidi and HarfBuzz, in every pack; the Unicode License v3 that
covers them is in each pack as C<LICENSES/Unicode-3.0.txt>.

The macOS and Linux packs carry no ImageMagick. conda-forge's libvips
depends on it, so it is in the environment those packs are cut from,
but they leave out libvips's ImageMagick loader and everything only it
needs — ImageMagick's own libraries, OpenEXR, and Graphviz, whose
EPL-1.0 is, on the Free Software Foundation's reading, incompatible
with the GPL code beside it. There is no C<magickload> or C<magicksave>
on those platforms, so files only ImageMagick reads do not open: BMP,
ICO and CUR, PSD, TGA, OpenEXR, DDS, DPX, PCX, QOI, SGI, Sun raster,
XBM, XPM, PAM, MIFF, MNG and JNG, MATLAB and VIFF files among them. The
Windows packs keep ImageMagick (under its own licence), and with it
all of those formats.

Where the packs come from:

=item B<macOS arm64> — the L<conda-forge|https://conda-forge.org/>
channel, platform C<osx-arm64>, libvips 8.18.7 build C<h588ea66_0>.
=item B<Linux x86_64> — conda-forge, C<linux-64>, libvips 8.18.7 build
C<he268db4_0>.
=item B<Linux aarch64> — conda-forge, C<linux-aarch64>, libvips 8.18.7
build C<h4590af0_0>.
=item B<Windows x86_64 and arm64> —
L<libvips/build-win64-mxe|https://github.com/libvips/build-win64-mxe>
release
L<v8.18.7|https://github.com/libvips/build-win64-mxe/releases/tag/v8.18.7>
(C<vips-dev-x64-all-8.18.7.zip>, C<vips-dev-arm64-all-8.18.7.zip>),
whose build recipe is that repository at that tag.

Each macOS and Linux pack's C<THIRD-PARTY.md> lists every package of
the environment the pack was cut from, by version and build string —
those that contribute no file to it too, ImageMagick among them (pinned
to conda-forge's C<imagemagick_*> builds, which declare ImageMagick's
own licence, rather than the C<agpl_*> builds of the same libraries) —
and, for every package in the pack, the conda-forge recipe at the
commit that built it and the upstream source archive.

To use no prebuilt at all, install with C<VIPS_NATIVE_PREFER_SYSTEM=1>:
nothing is downloaded, C<Vips::Native> loads the libvips you installed
yourself (see L<#INSTALLATION>), and the varargs shim is compiled from
its source with your C compiler. C<VIPS_NATIVE_LIB_DIR> points it at a
libvips of your own build instead. Either way it must be libvips 8.12 or
later. Platforms without a prebuilt
(including x86_64 macOS) always take that path.

=head1 EXTERNAL API

=head2 smart-resize

=begin code :lang<raku>
sub smart-resize(
    Str $in-path,
    Str $out-path,
    Int $out-width,
    Int $out-height,
    --> Bool
) is export
=end code

Smart-crop the source image to match the output aspect ratio (using
libvips's C<VIPS_INTERESTING_ATTENTION> saliency model — picks the
visually-important rectangle, not just the centre), then Lanczos3-
resize the cropped image to C<$out-width × $out-height> and save as
PNG to C<$out-path>.

Returns C<True> on success; C<fail>s with the underlying error if
libvips errors out.

A C<vips_smart_resize> shell binary is also installed:

  vips_smart_resize --in=/path/to/image.png --out=/path/to/save.png \
                    --width=180 --height=180

=head2 letterbox-to-buffer

=begin code :lang<raku>
sub letterbox-to-buffer(
    IO::Path() $path,
    UInt :$size = 448,
    :@bg = (255, 255, 255),
    --> Buf[uint8]
) is export
=end code

Resize C<$path> to fit inside a C<$size × $size> square while
preserving aspect ratio, pad the unfilled region with the C<@bg>
colour, composite alpha over that colour for RGBA inputs, and return
the raw 8-bit RGB pixel buffer in NHWC byte order. Length is always
exactly C<$size × $size × 3>.

The preprocess pipeline image classifiers want: deterministic gap
fill, fixed input dimensions, no GC entanglement with libvips's
heap. Pair with L<ONNX::Native> or your inference engine of choice
to feed RGB pixels directly.

The buffer is owned by the caller — internally we copy from the
C<vips_image_write_to_memory> result into a Raku-owned C<buf8> and
release the source via C<vips_shim_free>, so there's no lifetime
trap.

Inputs may be 3-band RGB or 4-band RGBA (UCHAR pixels only — 16-bit
PNG / HDR / float images are out of scope for v1). Greyscale and
CMYK fail loudly with details.

=head2 Vips::Native::FFI

The complete (variadic + non-variadic) libvips C bindings are
exported from C<Vips::Native::FFI>:

=item C<vips_init>, C<vips_image_new_from_file>
=item C<vips_image_get_width> / C<get_height> / C<get_bands> / C<get_format>
=item C<vips_smartcrop>, C<vips_resize>, C<vips_flatten>, C<vips_embed>
=item C<vips_pngsave>, C<vips_image_write_to_memory>
=item C<vips_shim_free> (libglib heap free for write-to-memory output)
=item C<g_object_unref> (release VipsImage handles)
=item C<VIPS_KERNEL_*>, C<VIPS_INTERESTING_*>, C<VIPS_EXTEND_*>,
C<VIPS_FORMAT_*> constants

C<vips_flatten> and C<vips_embed> require the libvips_shim (their
options take a C<VipsArrayDouble *> that's awkward to construct
directly from Raku). Every prebuilt bundle includes it; if you're
running against system libvips, the shim is compiled at install time
via the platform's C toolchain and falls back gracefully if no
toolchain is available.

A staged shim is kept only while it matches the shim source of the
release being installed: a C«libvips_shim.<ext>.srchash» file
beside it records the SHA-256 of the source it was built from. When
it does not match (a bundle cut before the source gained a function,
say), install refreshes it from a content-addressed cache, or
compiles it, and keeps the old one with a note only when it can do
neither. On Windows that compile uses a MinGW toolchain such as
MSYS2's UCRT64 (C<VIPS_NATIVE_CC> to choose another), links straight
against the bundle's DLLs, and replaces the staged shim only after
C<nm> shows it exports every C<vips_shim_*> function the source
defines.

